 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Coil operating range",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The coil-range graphs plot coil voltage, as a multiple of the rated coil voltage Urtd, against ambient temperature. Upper lines are drawn for 0 A, 2 × 4 A and 2 × 8 A contact current on DC coils and for 0 A and 2 × 8 A on AC coils; the DC range ends at 85 °C and the AC range at 70 °C. On DC coils the recommended voltage range runs from about 0.9 to 1.4 × Urtd."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Versions",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The product code gives the version (4: flux proof, RTII; E: wash tight, RTIII, not offered as reflow), the contacts (2: two changeover; 4: two make), the contact material (3: AgSnO, 4: AgNi 90/10, 5: AgNi 90/10 with gold plating), the coil code, and an R suffix for the through-hole-reflow version. RT424024 is the flux-proof, two-changeover, AgNi 90/10 relay with a 24 VDC coil."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reflow version",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The R version is made for through-hole reflow soldering, by forced convection or vapour phase but not infrared. It has a lower tracking index (PTI 175 V), a lower mechanical endurance (10 million operations for DC coils, 2 million for AC coils), a slightly taller body (16.2 mm) and shorter pins (3.1 mm), and reflow soldering can affect its contact performance."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Insulation",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Coil and contacts are separated by reinforced insulation rated 5 kV with at least 10 mm clearance and creepage. The relay meets IEC 60335-1."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Mounting",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "AC-coil relays need at least 2.5 mm between neighbouring relays. Contact ratings and endurance figures are for relays soldered to the board; relays fitted in sockets may need derating."
  }
 ],
